The desk merged date complaints into one known parser ticket. The tenant directory and the
running cause logic show a separate booking-form defect inside it. One parser fix will not
resolve both. This review replaces the old incident page's “one issue, one fix” conclusion,
not the underlying report history.

## Two causes, two tickets

<AuditSummary />

- **Parser:** the shared parser assumes month first even when the tenant writes day first.
  An app report from Harbour Dental, `R-41200`, says 7 April became 4 July.
  [Parser issue](https://github.com/keyroll-99/mega-dev-s01e03/issues/1).
- **Hint:** booking-v3 prints `MM/DD/YYYY`, while the form interprets input in the tenant's
  day-first convention. `R-41572` from Copper Studio says 7 May became 5 July.
  [Booking-form hint issue](https://github.com/keyroll-99/mega-dev-s01e03/issues/2).

These mechanisms require separate dispositions; a parser fix alone does not fix a misleading
form hint. The engineering update below now records a fix for parser and retirement for hint.

## Engineering update · 5 October 2026

The [Challenge page](https://hq.mega.dev/challenges/what-it-shows) now reports source
**#4471 closed on 5 October 2026**, after the 4 October export snapshot:

| Cause | Desk resolution | Reason | GitHub closure |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| parser | **FIXED** | `fix(dates): parse dd/mm for eu locales` | [Issue #1](https://github.com/keyroll-99/mega-dev-s01e03/issues/1), completed |
| hint | **WON'T FIX** | booking-v3 form retired | [Issue #2](https://github.com/keyroll-99/mega-dev-s01e03/issues/2), not planned |

Both tickets are closed, but only the parser was fixed. Retirement removed the affected hint
form; it is not a delivered hint repair. These outcomes are the desk's reported engineering
update, not an independent production verification. Counts and the table below remain an
audit of the historical export, not current unresolved backlog.

## What the data says, not what intake guessed

<CauseTable ticket={4471} />

The table uses distinct report IDs, then `mergedInto`, then tenant locale, form version and
channel through `causeOf`. The browser's locale and `meta.intakeTag` are intake guesses,
not the tenant's date contract. For a day-first tenant on booking-v3, form reports belong
to hint; app and email reports still belong to parser. Booking-v2 form reports belong to parser.
Unknown tenants must remain unplaced until investigated, not merged by matching text.

<Callout kind="warn">
`R-41202`, `R-41209`, `R-41236`, `R-41344`, and `R-41620` describe one-day moves, an invite
before booking, or different dates across cities. All remain counted under parser, as the
supplied classifier and desk notes file them. Their bodies are not explained by a simple
month/day swap. Preserve the evidence and investigate; a third cause is not yet established.
</Callout>

## Receipt time is not merge time

<QueueTimeline ticket={4471} from="2026-03-30" to="2026-11-02" />

Reports arrived throughout April, May and June before a bulk merge in July. The received bars
show when people reported trouble; the merged bars show desk activity. A merge spike is not
an onset spike, and “known” is not “fixed”. Later arrivals show why the desk had to keep reviewing
new evidence while the repair waited. The export ends on 4 October: it cannot establish a
resolution after that date. The later engineering update above supplies the separate outcomes.

## Change the rule the desk uses

The accepted [decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) is historical and remains
unchanged. Its subject-only rule conflicts with the running cause logic and is far beyond
the KB's review threshold. A recent review date on the old incident cannot make that rule safe.

The new [decision 0002](/kb/decisions/0002-dedupe-by-cause) replaces that rule for this KB.
The [triage page](/kb/triage/dedupe) now directs the desk to compare tenant configuration,
channel and mechanism, reading bodies for contradictions.

<DedupeExplorer />

Same subject can hide different causes: Alder Physio form versus app.
Different subjects can describe the same cause: Kettle Books app versus email.
A matching subject is a search aid, never proof that reports are duplicates.

## How to verify and keep this current

Run `npm ci`, `npm run analyse`, `npm test`, and `npm run build`.
The source of computed values is `src/lib/data`; classification stays in `src/lib/kb`.
`evidence/analysis.json` records report IDs for each issue. The built page is preserved under
`rendered/kb/incidents/4471-review/index.html`, alongside its shared assets.

When reports, tenant configuration or desk notes change, rerun the analysis, reconcile issue
counts, re-read uncertain bodies and rebuild the page. Do not copy an old rendered count into
new prose. This page is signed by the GitHub owner working with Codex; the desk reference below
is computed by `RenderStamp` from the signature, not typed in.

